rck and his detective squad the books  have appeared in 36 countries each  german edition has been a best seller  they've sold millions of copies in total  you can't enter the best selling list  very easily in countries where they  speak english only because they prefer  their own  but here in germany all of us are  competing on the same level  same best-selling list for everyone  throughout the world and if if you can  enter that  you actually can enter the whole world  the worldwide boom got its start with  swedish author henning mankell in the  1990s his thrillers took on weighty  topics like africa politics and  xenophobia at home  he was followed by many more like lisa  markland  another swedish star was stig larson who  died in 2004.  nowadays scandinavian crime fiction is  the hottest game in town  dubious gauless is a journalist book  critic and member of the jury for the  german crime fiction award he keeps a  close eye on the trends  in a sense these scandinavian novels are  like a mirror for european and german  readers  scandinavians feel certain things more  keenly than we do the countries the  societies actually have more security  than we do here  so they respond hysterically when this  security is threatened  their writing takes on this collective  fear this sense of anxiety which exists  here too  in dramatic form  scandinavia may present an unruffled  surface to the casual observer but  underneath it's teeming with bleak  plotlines and angst-ridden protagonists  nordic noir where little is what it  seems  germans feel an affinity with the  scandinavians and are fascinated by  their society their crime novels tend to  focus on social issues it's often said  that crime novels have replaced regular  fiction as a genre in terms of observing  society  this latest installment won't be the  last  adler olsen is planning another five  books in his department q series  and even in germany you see adler olsen  enjoys giving a reading in the original  danish 