is project gave us lang lang from  china  in collaboration with simon rydel from  britain with the philharmonic in berlin  an international orchestra  that's typical of dwc  and a perfect topic for us  the film depicts the process of  recording two pioneering works of the  20th century  piano concerto number three by russian  composer sergey prokofiev and piano  concerto number two by his hungarian  contemporary bela bartok  let's just do a take shall we see what  yeah so  it's a very musical  clips  and also  it was a real good documentary  there are a lot of comments from  different parts of the teammates  for this project i think will be very  helpful  to people who don't know so much about  how we're making  recordings  and this is  a real team work to make  the best we can  the cd is already out the film will be  released on dvd and blu-ray in early  november  you can tune in to dw on november 30th  to see the documentary in german english  spanish or arabic  well every year in october berlin hosts  a two-week-long festival of lights that  floods the city's monuments and  buildings in color and light after  sundown theresa mar is an austrian light  artist from vienna who is now a veteran  at this event and our reporter caught up  with her to see what she had in store  for the german capital this year  teresa mar at the dress rehearsal the  evening before the festival of lights  opens  it's only when the slides are projected  onto the building that the artist knows  if all the months of work have paid off  wow  looks great it's there for the first  time and it's totally new for me a big  surprise  ma has completed eight light collages  for the pallayam festoons grab  festungsgraben in central berlin she  wants her work called prisma to change  the perception of the building  the substance of my work begins with  white light  when it falls on raindrops you have a  rainbow it makes you want to look twice  and that's how it is with this building  this projection will certainly make  berliners look at it closely  theresa ma works intuitively she says  her images fit together like a puzzle  first she creates a collage from 200 to  300 paper snippets that she's cut out of  magazines  the finished image is scanned  she then edits and processes it on her  computer till she's satisfied  theresa mars says she is a painter who  uses different ways to present her art  her canvas involves public spaces and  buildings which he paints in bright  colors  you obviously have to have a talent and  drive in implementing things  without it you can't have a profession  like this  i can imagine how an image can appear in  a larger dimension  ma presented her first work in vienna in  2006.  her international breakthrough came at  the glow lights festival in the  netherlands in 2010  a year later she brightened up berlin  cathedral at the festival of lights  in 2012 her installation sinfonia lit up  the concert house at berlin's  jean-darmanmarkt  this year her images can be seen on two  buildings together with other artists  teresamar has once again illuminated  berlin cathedral  in berlin  berlin has huge buildings and sprawling  squares  this city has a feel for space it's  perfect for a light projection and  this is perfect for an elite project  zone the festival of lights is to  brighten the german capital for 12  nights famous artworks give a new look  to potsdamer platz  the brandenburg gate has huge  three-dimensional images known as video  mappings  and the facade of the hotel de rome  boasts some old masters  the lights festival has already become a  tourist magnet organizers expect around  2 million visitors  gorgeous colors  amazing it changes the building's  character and makes it more cheerful  looks very spectacular now  rainbow colors everywhere  i simply like it when you see so many  lights and colors in the night you don't  notice that it's cold and windy in that  it's autumn  helps this  these light images are like snapshots  for teresa martin's part of her concept  she wants viewers to reflect for a  moment and keep the image in their  memories  nyx has come to see the next thing is  i'll be illuminating the emitage in  amsterdam  that's a 100 meter long facade and i'll  be using a moving image  and then we'll see if i'm back in berlin  next year  and otherwise there are a few buildings  in the world that fascinate me and draw  me and that i would like to illuminate  someday  one of her dreams is to light up the  philharmonic in luxembourg and to bring  art to the streets there too  the festival of lights does add a splash  of color to berlin's autumn nights  well its ice is almost twice as thick as  the eiffel tower is high the monumental  yostada glacier in norway and with a  surface area of almost 500 square  kilometers it's the largest glacier on  continental europe as you can see here  it drapes itself over three large fjords  in southwestern norway about 300  kilometers northeast of bergen and it's  one of that area's major tourist  attractions and we met the only woman  who can take you across it  the norwegian glacier yostada's plane is  a colossus of snow and ice  it's the star attraction here in yostata  valley  heidi zimmerman who originally hails  from switzerland has been working here  since 1990 guiding people around  continental europe's largest glacier  my name is heidi  and i wish you all welcome on the  glacier it will come one more guide his  name is arno  the tour kicks off with a 30 minute boat  trip across the glacial lake  a chance for the group to catch their  breath ahead of the ascent to nigard's  plane a sidearm of yostata's plane  you see those people those tiny dots up  there we'll be going higher than them  you see that ridge which crosses the  valley  we'll be going about as far as that  each year heidi takes about 10 000  people on tours around the glacier  today's group is an international bunch  as well as norwegians there are  australian dutch and german tourists ice  climbing is a popular pastime but that's  nothing new the first norwegian  mountaineers ventured out onto the  yostatasplain in 1880 their equipment  was pretty basic a canvas rook sack a  stick and light cotton clothing but they  approached the endeavor in a very  similar manner rope together in a group  and of course i will check everything  safety is very important but i will not  check before i put you in rope because  then i have control  that i can check everyone  heidi knows exactly what to look out for  mountaineering is her passion  in switzerland she was the second woman  to become a certified mountain guide  she's a pioneering figure in norway too  the only female mountain guide in the  whole country  and even after twenty three years she  says she never gets bored on the  yoshita's plane  conditions change every day on the  glacier it moves up to a meter a day  it can be transformed in a week  it's always a challenge to find a new  path  i like having to constantly respond to  new people you can do more with some of  them with others  the day tour lasts five hours and ends  at a very special place  at the foot of the glacier where water  rushes into the valley at 10 000 liters  a second  it's a beautiful but treacherous spot  this  it's a wonderful blue color and so  people are tempted to go very close  but if that breaks away it'll dam up the  whole river  that will cause a huge flood wave  capable of washing people away so i see  it as the most dangerous place of any  glacier much more dangerous than  clevases  as soon as she's back in yostata valley  heidi heads off to the mountains again  this time she's accompanied by her dogs  the mountains are her refuge  of course climbing on your own is  different than when you are responsible  for other people  you don't have to make concessions  you can choose your own company  so i go on more difficult tours than i  would with visitors i love being in the  mountains more than anywhere else  and in a way that's hardly surprising  for someone whose name is heidi and who  comes from the swiss alps  and that brings us to the end of this  edition of our euromax highlights i hope  you enjoyed it and until we meet again  all the best from us here in berlin  you

