r teresa martin's part of her concept  she wants viewers to reflect for a  moment and keep the image in their  memories  nyx has come to see the next thing is  i'll be illuminating the emitage in  amsterdam  that's a 100 meter long facade and i'll  be using a moving image  and then we'll see if i'm back in berlin  next year  and otherwise there are a few buildings  in the world that fascinate me and draw  me and that i would like to illuminate  someday  one of her dreams is to light up the  philharmonic in luxembourg and to bring  art to the streets there too  the festival of lights does add a splash  of color to berlin's autumn nights  well its ice is almost twice as thick as  the eiffel tower is high the monumental  yostada glacier in norway and with a  surface area of almost 500 square  kilometers it's the largest glacier on  continental europe as you can see here  it drapes itself over three large fjords  in southwestern norway about 300  kilometers northeast of bergen and it's  one of that area's major tourist  attractions and we met the only woman  who can take you across it  the norwegian glacier yostada's plane is  a colossus of snow and ice  it's the star attraction here in yostata  valley  heidi zimmerman who originally hails  from switzerland has been working here  since 1990 guiding people around  continental europe's largest glacier  my name is heidi  and i wish you all welcome on the  glacier it will come one more guide his  name is arno  the tour kicks off with a 30 minute boat  trip across the glacial lake  a chance for the group to catch their  breath ahead of the ascent to nigard's  plane a sidearm of yostata's plane  you see those people those tiny dots up  there we'll be going higher than them  you see that ridge which crosses the  valley  we'll be going about as far as that  each year heidi takes about 10 000  people on tours around the glacier  today's group is an international bunch  as well as norwegians there are  australian dutch and german tourists ice  climbing is a popular pastime but that's  nothing new the first norwegian  mountaineers ventured out onto the  yostatasplain in 1880 their equipment  was pretty basic a canvas rook sack a  stick and light cotton clothing but they  approached the endeavor in a very  similar manner rope together in a group  and of course i will check everything  safety is very important but i will not  check before i put you in rope because  then i have control  that i can check everyone  heidi knows exactly what to look out for  mountaineering is her passion  in switzerland she was the second woman  to become a certified mountain guide  she's a pioneering figure in norway too  the only female mountain guide in the  whole country  and even after twenty three years she  says she never gets bored on the  yoshita's plane  conditions change every day on the  glacier it moves up to a meter a day  it can be transformed in a week  it's always a challenge to find a new  path  i like having to constantly respond to  new people you can do more with some of  them with others  the day tour lasts five hours and ends  at a very special place  at the foot of the glacier where water  rushes into the valley at 10 000 liters  a second  it's a beautiful but treacherous spot  this  it's a wonderful blue color and so  people are tempted to go very close  but if that breaks away it'll dam up the  whole river  that will cause a huge flood wave  capable of washing people away so i see  it as the most dangerous place of any  glacier much more dangerous than  clevases  as soon as she's back in yostata valley  heidi heads off to the mountains again  this time she's accompanied by her dogs  the mountains are her refuge  of course climbing on your own is  different than when you are responsible  for other people  you don't have to make concessions  you can choose your own company  so i go on more difficult tours than i  would with visitors i love being in the  mountains more than anywhere else  and in a way that's hardly surprising  for someone whose name is heidi and who  comes from the swiss alps  and that brings us to the end of this  edition of our euromax highlights i hope  you enjoyed it and until we meet again  all the best from us here in berlin  you
 

